{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Take the staff and assemble the congregation, etc.</b> If HaShem commanded him to speak to the rock, then what need has he for the staff? And furthermore, it did not say 'take your staff' but said 'the staff' — infer from this that it is a specific, well-known staff that is meant. And as for what it says 'from before HaShem' — now did the staff of Moses stand before HaShem? And why did He call them 'rebels' specifically in connection with this incident? Now behold, as for the essence of this sin, eleven opinions have been set forth, which Rabbi Isaac (Mahari'a) recorded in his book, and there you will find many opinions far from the plain sense. And now, look to the right and see, and set your heart toward my opinion." "And know and understand that I found in the Chizkuni that this staff was the staff of Aaron which had been placed before HaShem. And his words are unseasoned, for he did not explain what the nature of Aaron's staff was in this context. And I say that in truth the language proves that this staff was Aaron's staff, since it is written 'the staff,' meaning the well-known staff that had been placed for safekeeping before HaShem. And it was dry wood that had no moisture of water whatsoever, yet it nevertheless produced blossom and flower — and there is no blossom and flower without water, except that the Holy One, blessed be He, decreed upon it that it should produce water, and HaShem brought dry wood to bloom by means of the water that it produced through the decree of El, blessed be He. And the Holy One, blessed be He, wanted Moses to take Aaron's staff and show it to all Israel, to say: just as HaShem brought water out of this dry wood, so will He bring water out of this dry rock of flint. And what it says, 'and you shall speak to the rock and it shall give its water' — for thus you shall speak to the rock, that it should do just as this staff did. And since the text of what Moses was to say to the rock was not specified in Scripture, surely the formula is the two words 'וְנָתַן מֵימָיו,' for 'וְנָתַן' is in the past tense, meaning that he would speak to the rock about the matter of the staff, that it produced blossom and gave its waters — for with any blossom and flower there is moisture of water — and when the rock hears that the staff has already given its waters, then it too will do like it, and through this 'and you shall bring forth for them water from the rock.' A hint to the matter: 'סֶלַע' in gematria equals 'עֵץ,' to teach from the dry wood to do as its deed. But He did not command to strike it at all. And when Moses struck it, it is written 'בְּמַטֵּהוּ,' meaning he struck with his own staff and not with Aaron's staff. 'And Moses took the staff from before HaShem as He commanded him' — for he understood this from the fact that it said 'the staff,' the well-known one, and did not say 'your staff'; and Scripture informs us that only this act of taking was as HaShem commanded him, but what he did from that point onward he did not do as HaShem commanded him." "And the nature of the sin of striking is as follows. For we find among our Sages of blessed memory (Shemot Rabbah 21:9) regarding the splitting of the Sea of Reeds, that it is written (Exodus 14:16): 'And you — raise your staff and stretch out your hand.' Now this 'raising' is an expression of removal and elevation: 'Raise your staff' — remove it; 'and stretch out your hand' — and not with your staff. This was because they were murmuring about the staff, saying that all the signs that Moses performed with his staff were all done by means of some sorcery that resided in this staff, for we see that his power lies only in this staff. Therefore it says 'Raise your staff' in order to remove this notion from their hearts. And therefore it says (Exodus 14:31), 'And Israel saw the great hand,' meaning that they saw that this sign was done by the hand and not by the staff; therefore 'and they believed in HaShem and in Moses His servant' — by inference, until now they were children in whom there was no faith, for they did not believe in HaShem and in the prophetic office of Moses His servant, because they had grounds to attribute the signs to the staff." "Now at this time, many days had passed for Israel since the sea was split, and this generation did not know whether Moses performed that sign by his hand or by his staff, and they assumed that just as all the signs in Egypt were done with the staff, so too the splitting of the Sea of Reeds. And by striking the rock with the staff, he brought Israel back into a deficiency of faith, for they reverted and said that it was not HaShem who accomplished all this, but rather through his staff there is some aspect of sorcery. There, at the sea, it says 'And you — raise your staff and stretch out your hand': the language of raising and removal for the staff, and the language of stretching for the hand, and through this 'and they believed in HaShem.' But here it says the precise opposite: 'And Moses raised his hand and struck with his staff' — the language of raising and removal for the hand, for since he did not perform this act by merely stretching out his hand, it is as if he removed his hand and struck with his staff. And through this he restored a modicum of faithlessness to its original place, as it was in the murmuring about the staff before the splitting of the sea. Therefore it says 'יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי' — 'לֹא אֲמַנְתֶּם' was not said, but rather 'הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם,' which is a form bearing a twofold sense, that they caused others not to believe in Me, but rather to say: by the power of the staff he did this, and not HaShem who accomplished all this. And in this they committed two evils: for they did not speak to the rock at all — and Aaron too was liable in this matter, for it says 'and you shall speak to the rock' — and had they spoken to the rock about the matter of Aaron's staff and what it had done, and the rock would have heard and done as its deed, there is no greater sanctification of the Name than this, for all would learn an a fortiori argument from the rock. Now not only did they not speak at all so as to uphold the faith, but he struck it with his staff and brought weakness into the faith. And this is what it says: 'יַעַן לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם' — 'in Me' — you did not cause others to believe in Me." "And if you should say: is not the statement 'Shall flocks and cattle be slaughtered for them' (Numbers 11:22) more severe than this? — concerning this He said 'to sanctify Me before the eyes of the children of Israel,' for that was in private and this was in public before the eyes of all Israel. For such is the measure regarding desecration of the Name: all who desecrate the Name of Heaven in private are punished publicly (Avot 4:5) — meaning that the Holy One, blessed be He, brings it about in his hand that he sin publicly, so that people will know why He, blessed be He, exacts punishment from him. And as for why He began with 'the children of Israel' and afterward said 'therefore you shall not bring the congregation': thus the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses — if all those entering the land were children of Israel, children of Abraham, of whom it is said (Genesis 15:6) 'and he believed in HaShem,' I would remain silent, for since they are believers, children of believers, even if you do not perform before them signs that sustain the faith, they will nevertheless remain strong in faith and will not turn away from it. However, among those who enter the land there is a congregation of converts — they are the mixed multitude who are not of the children of Israel, for in adversity they will easily revert to their former state, as they are children in whom there is no faith. Therefore those who enter the land need a leader who can go against the spirit of each and every one, and who performs before them constantly signs that sustain the faith. And you — it is not your way to be insistent on this; therefore 'you shall not bring the congregation' — that congregation of converts — into the land." "And therefore it is said concerning Joshua (Deuteronomy 31:7): ״לְעֵינֵי כָל יִשְׂרָאֵל חֲזַק וֶאֱמָץ כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא אֶת הָעָם הַזֶּה אֶל הָאָרֶץ״ — there too it begins with ״יִשְׂרָאֵל״ and concludes with ״עָם״, for He said: accustom yourself, before the eyes of all Israel, to be strong and resolute in the word of HaShem, so that they not come through you to a diminution of faith. And if you say that Israel are not suspect in your eyes, concerning this He said: ״כִּי אַתָּה תָּבִיא אֶת הָעָם הַזֶּה״ — for among them is also the mixed multitude, who are called by the name ״הָעָם״, and they require strengthening all the more. And this matter will be explained further below in parashat Vayelekh with the help of HaShem. And this interpretation is more precious than all that the commentators have said concerning it, as your eyes will see in the book of Maharia." "And I will add further and speak concerning what is written: ״וַיַּךְ אֶת הַסֶּלַע בְּמַטֵּהוּ פַּעֲמָיִם״ — from this there ensued a diminution of Israel's faith, that they would not heed the voice of HaShem and the voice of His prophets except after many blows, for ״תֵּחַת גְּעָרָה בְמֵבִין מֵהַכּוֹת כְּסִיל מֵאָה״ (Proverbs 17:10), as derived in the Yalkut, siman 763, and this is its language: ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם אֶל הַסֶּלַע״ — ״וְהִכִּיתֶם״ is not said, but rather ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם״: when the lad is small, his teacher teaches him and strikes him. Once he has grown, he disciplines him with speech. So the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: when the rock was small you struck it, as it is written: ״וְהִכִּיתָ בַצּוּר״, but now that it has grown — ״וְדִבַּרְתֶּם אֶל הַסֶּלַע״. Now every intelligent person will wonder at the spectacle — does this rock have eyes of flesh? Is it not the same there, small and great being all one? How did He compare it to a lad? Surely the intent is only toward the moral lesson, that Israel should learn from it, for there is a difference between discipline by word of mouth and discipline by the rod of the taskmaster, for the one who desists under compulsion will return to his disobedience, as will be explained with the help of HaShem below in parashat Vayelekh. And the Holy One, blessed be He, showed us that even though this matter does not apply to the rock, nevertheless in order to demonstrate it, these details were brought, to say that it is not proper to strike with the rod except for a lad and a fool, but once he has grown — ״תֵּחַת גְְּעָרָה בְמֵבִין״; for if he does not heed the voice of discipline and must be struck with afflictions, without doubt as soon as relief appears he will revert to his disobedience — just as it was necessary to strike the rock twice, for it did not obey at the first blow. So too with everything done by way of blows and compulsion, the effect of the deed is not accomplished swiftly. And this is the profanation of the Name — that the children of Israel would learn from it not to heed the voice of discipline, but only after the beating of a fool a hundred times. And this is a very fitting allusion according to the opinion of this midrash."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:52.036277113Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}